 We are given that the areas of the two circles are $\pi$ and $4\pi$ and that the total area of the shaded region is $\frac{5}{12}$ of the area of the larger circle. Depending on $\angle ADC$, the area that is shaded either consists of the area of the sector of the larger circle minus the area of a triangle $Ach$ or the area of a sector of the smaller plus the area of a triangle $ABc$. Since the angle of a full circle is $2\pi$, the proportion of the area that is shaded ($\frac{5}{12}$) is just $\frac{\angle ADC}{2\pi}$. Putting all this together, we see that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\angle ADC}{2\pi} &= \frac{5}{12} - \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{AD}{AC}\right).
\end{align*}
Thus, 
\begin{align*}
\angle ADC &= \left(\frac{5}{12} - \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{AD}{AC}\right)\right) \times 2\pi.
\end{align*}
Since $\angle ADC$ is inscribed in the larger circle, it only has measure $120^\circ$ or $\frac{2\pi}{3}$, so $\sin\left(\frac{AD}{AC}\right) = \sin\left(\frac{AC - AD}{AC}\right) = \sin\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)$. Thus, $\angle ADC = \left(\frac{5}{12} - \sin^{-1}\left(\sin\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)\right)\right) \times 2\pi = \frac{1}{3} \times 2\pi = \boxed{\frac{2\pi}{3}}$. </think> <answer> 
